med("overactive-bladder-medicines", "Oxybutynin, Solifenacin and Mirabegron: Overactive Bladder Medicines",
 "Overactive bladder medicines explained: anticholinergics (oxybutynin, solifenacin) and mirabegron, how well they work, dry mouth and memory concerns in older people, and blood pressure.",
 "Overactive bladder medicines", "Overactive bladder medicines",
 [("s", "Anticholinergics and beta-3 agonists"), ("l", "Prescription only (S4)")],
 "For urgency, frequency and leaks from an overactive bladder, after bladder training. Mirabegron avoids the memory concerns of older anticholinergics.",
 "These medicines calm bladder muscle contractions. Bladder training, pelvic floor exercises and fluid adjustments come first. Anticholinergics like oxybutynin cause dry mouth and constipation and may affect memory in older people; mirabegron works differently but can raise blood pressure.",
 [("For", "Overactive bladder: urgency, frequency, night-time urination, urge leaks"),
  ("Works", "Modest: about 1 to 2 fewer urgency episodes or leaks a day"),
  COST,
  ("Kicks in", "Within 2 weeks; full effect by 8 to 12 weeks"),
  ("Dose", "Oxybutynin 2.5 to 5 mg two to three times daily (or patch); solifenacin 5 to 10 mg daily; mirabegron 25 to 50 mg daily"),
  ("Watch for", "Dry mouth, constipation, confusion (anticholinergics); raised blood pressure (mirabegron)")],
 "Calms the bladder, not the cause.",
 ["Oxybutynin: Ditropan, Oxytrol (patch). Solifenacin: Vesicare. Darifenacin: Enablex. Tolterodine: Detrusitol. Mirabegron: Betmiga. Vibegron: Beova"],
 "Anticholinergics block muscarinic receptors on bladder muscle; mirabegron and vibegron stimulate beta-3 receptors that relax it.",
 ["Overactive bladder", "Urge incontinence", "Night-time urination (with other measures)", "Bladder spasms after surgery or with catheters"],
 "Anticholinergic effects add up with other medicines that have them.",
 [g(V, ["Dry mouth (anticholinergics)"]), g(C, ["Constipation", "Blurred vision", "UTIs"]), g(U, ["<b>Confusion, memory problems</b> in older people (anticholinergics)", "Raised blood pressure (mirabegron)", "Trouble emptying the bladder"]), g(R, ["Acute glaucoma (anticholinergics)", "Fast heartbeat"])],
 ["Unable to pass urine", "Sudden eye pain with blurred vision", "New confusion"],
 ["Try bladder training and pelvic floor exercises first or alongside.", "Older people and those at risk of dementia: discuss mirabegron or vibegron.", "Check blood pressure on mirabegron.", "Review after 3 months: stop if not helping."],
 [("Does oxybutynin cause dementia?", "Long-term use of strongly anticholinergic medicines has been linked with higher dementia risk in studies. It’s an association, but many doctors prefer alternatives in older people."),
  ("Which overactive bladder medicine is best?", "They work about equally well. The choice depends on side effects: mirabegron and vibegron avoid dry mouth and memory concerns."),
  ("How long before bladder medicines work?", "Some improvement within 2 weeks, with full benefit by 8 to 12 weeks.")],
 [("Continence Health Australia", "https://www.continence.org.au/"), ("PBS: Mirabegron", PBS + "mirabegron")],
 [("Bladder leaks", "/bladder-leaks/")])

med("methotrexate", "Methotrexate: Weekly Dosing, Folic Acid, Blood Tests and Safety",
 "Methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis and other conditions explained: once-WEEKLY dosing, folic acid, blood tests, alcohol, interactions (trimethoprim), pregnancy and infections.",
 "Methotrexate", "Methotrexate",
 [("s", "Disease-modifying medicine (DMARD)"), ("l", "Prescription only (S4)")],
 "The cornerstone of rheumatoid arthritis treatment. Taken ONCE A WEEK: daily dosing by mistake can be fatal.",
 "Low-dose methotrexate calms the immune system in rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis and other conditions. It’s taken once weekly, with folic acid on another day. Regular blood tests check blood counts, liver and kidneys. It’s harmful in pregnancy.",
 [("For", "Rheumatoid and psoriatic arthritis, psoriasis, some vasculitis, Crohn’s disease, other autoimmune conditions"),
  ("Works", "Improves symptoms and slows joint damage in most people with RA"),
  COST,
  ("Kicks in", "6 to 12 weeks"),
  ("Dose", "Usually 7.5 to 25 mg ONCE A WEEK (tablets or injection), plus folic acid on a different day"),
  ("Watch for", "Nausea, mouth ulcers, tiredness, infections")],
 "Once a week. Never daily.",
 ["Methoblastin, Trexject, Metoject (injections)"],
 "At low doses it has anti-inflammatory effects (partly via adenosine) and reduces overactive immune cells.",
 ["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Psoriatic arthritis and psoriasis", "Polymyalgia and vasculitis (steroid-sparing)", "Crohn’s disease", "Ectopic pregnancy and cancers (different doses and settings)"],
 "Folic acid reduces many side effects.",
 [g(C, ["Nausea, especially the day after", "Mouth ulcers", "Tiredness", "Raised liver tests"]), g(U, ["Hair thinning", "Low blood counts", "Infections"]), g(R, ["<b>Lung inflammation</b> (cough, breathlessness)", "<b>Severe bone marrow suppression</b>, especially with trimethoprim or overdose"])],
 ["New cough, fever or breathlessness", "Sore throat, mouth ulcers and bruising together", "Accidentally taken more than once in a week: seek advice today"],
 ["Take on the same day each week; mark it on a calendar.", "Avoid trimethoprim and co-trimoxazole; check new medicines with your pharmacist.", "Keep alcohol low.", "Plan pregnancy with your specialist: methotrexate is stopped beforehand (both partners should discuss).", "Get the recommended vaccines; avoid live vaccines unless advised."],
 [("Why is methotrexate taken weekly?", "At weekly doses it calms the immune system safely. Taking the same dose daily can cause life-threatening toxicity."),
  ("Why do I take folic acid with methotrexate?", "Folic acid reduces nausea, mouth ulcers and liver effects, without reducing benefit. Take it on a different day from methotrexate."),
  ("Can I drink alcohol on methotrexate?", "Small amounts may be OK for some people, but both affect the liver. Ask your specialist.")],
 [("Australian Rheumatology Association: Patient information", "https://rheumatology.org.au/For-Patients/Medication-Information"), ("PBS: Methotrexate", PBS + "methotrexate")],
 [("Rheumatoid arthritis", "/rheumatoid/"), ("Psoriasis", "/psoriasis/"), ("Hydroxychloroquine", "/medicines/hydroxychloroquine/")])

med("hydroxychloroquine", "Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil): Uses, Eye Checks and Side Effects",
 "Hydroxychloroquine explained: lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, how long it takes, eye (retina) screening, dose limits by weight, side effects and safety in pregnancy.",
 "Hydroxychloroquine", "Hydroxychloroquine",
 [("s", "Antimalarial DMARD"), ("l", "Prescription only (S4)")],
 "A gentle, long-term immune modulator for lupus and arthritis. Safe for most, with yearly eye checks after 5 years.",
 "Hydroxychloroquine reduces flares and organ damage in lupus and helps rheumatoid arthritis, usually alongside other medicines. It works slowly. The main long-term risk is damage to the retina, which is rare if the dose stays at or below 5 mg/kg of actual body weight a day and screening is done.",
 [("For", "Lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, Sjögren’s, some skin conditions"),
  ("Works", "Fewer lupus flares and better survival"),
  COST,
  ("Kicks in", "2 to 3 months, full effect up to 6 months"),
  ("Dose", "Usually 200 to 400 mg daily, no more than 5 mg/kg actual body weight"),
  ("Watch for", "Nausea, rash, vision changes")],
 "Slow, steady and kind to most.",
 ["Plaquenil, Hydroxychloroquine generics"],
 "It accumulates in immune cells and dampens signals (such as toll-like receptors) that drive autoimmune inflammation.",
 ["Systemic lupus erythematosus (recommended for nearly all)", "Rheumatoid arthritis (often combined)", "Sjögren’s syndrome", "Cutaneous lupus and some other skin conditions"],
 "Generally well tolerated.",
 [g(C, ["Nausea, diarrhoea", "Headache", "Rash"]), g(U, ["Skin darkening", "Low blood sugar"]), g(R, ["<b>Retinal damage</b> (risk rises after 5 years and above recommended doses)", "Heart rhythm problems (QT prolongation)", "Severe skin reactions"])],
 ["Any change in vision", "Fainting or palpitations", "Blistering rash"],
 ["Have a baseline eye check, then yearly screening after 5 years (earlier with risk factors).", "Take with food.", "Generally continued in pregnancy for lupus: it lowers flare risk.", "Keep out of reach of children: overdose is very dangerous."],
 [("Why do I need eye tests on hydroxychloroquine?", "Long-term use can damage the retina. Screening finds it early, before vision is affected."),
  ("Is hydroxychloroquine safe in pregnancy?", "Yes, for lupus it’s usually continued, as stopping raises the risk of flares."),
  ("How long does hydroxychloroquine take to work?", "Usually 2 to 3 months, sometimes up to 6.")],
 [("Australian Rheumatology Association: Patient information", "https://rheumatology.org.au/For-Patients/Medication-Information"), ("PBS: Hydroxychloroquine", PBS + "hydroxychloroquine")],
 [("Lupus", "/lupus/"), ("Rheumatoid arthritis", "/rheumatoid/"), ("Methotrexate", "/medicines/methotrexate/")])

med("ondansetron", "Ondansetron (Zofran): Nausea and Vomiting Uses and Side Effects",
 "Ondansetron explained: nausea and vomiting from gastro, chemotherapy, surgery and pregnancy, wafers and tablets, constipation, headache, heart rhythm and pregnancy safety.",
 "Ondansetron", "Ondansetron",
 [("s", "5-HT3 antagonist anti-nausea"), ("l", "Prescription only (S4)")],
 "A highly effective anti-nausea medicine, often as a melt-in-the-mouth wafer. Common in emergency departments for gastro in children.",
 "Ondansetron blocks serotonin signals that trigger vomiting. A single dose helps children with gastro keep fluids down and avoid a drip. It’s used for nausea from chemotherapy, surgery and severe pregnancy sickness.",
 [("For", "Nausea and vomiting: gastro, chemotherapy, after surgery, pregnancy (hyperemesis)"),
  ("Works", "Very effective for vomiting"),
  ("Cost", "PBS for chemotherapy-related use; otherwise private, around $15 to $30"),
  ("Kicks in", "Within 30 minutes"),
  ("Dose", "Adults 4 to 8 mg up to every 8 hours; children by weight"),
  ("Watch for", "Constipation, headache")],
 "Stops the spew.",
 ["Zofran, Ondaz, Onsetron, generics (tablets and wafers)"],
 "It blocks 5-HT3 receptors in the gut and the brain’s vomiting centre.",
 ["Gastroenteritis (single dose in children to help oral fluids)", "Chemotherapy and radiotherapy nausea", "After surgery", "Severe nausea and vomiting in pregnancy"],
 "Usually mild.",
 [g(C, ["Constipation", "Headache", "Feeling warm or flushed"]), g(U, ["Dizziness", "Hiccups"]), g(R, ["<b>Heart rhythm changes</b> (QT prolongation), mainly at high IV doses or with other QT drugs", "Serotonin syndrome with other serotonergic drugs"])],
 ["Signs of dehydration: little urine, drowsy, sunken eyes (especially in children)", "Palpitations or fainting", "Vomiting blood or green fluid"],
 ["Wafers dissolve on the tongue: no water needed.", "Keep up small, frequent sips of oral rehydration fluid.", "Pregnancy: large studies are mostly reassuring; any very small risk of cleft palate in early pregnancy should be discussed.", "Tell your doctor about heart rhythm problems or other QT-prolonging medicines."],
 [("Is ondansetron safe in pregnancy?", "It’s widely used for severe pregnancy sickness. Large studies are mostly reassuring, with a possible very small increase in cleft palate when used in the first trimester. Discuss with your doctor."),
  ("Can children take ondansetron for gastro?", "Yes, a single weight-based dose often stops vomiting long enough to rehydrate by mouth."),
  ("Does ondansetron cause constipation?", "Yes, it’s one of the most common side effects.")],
 [("PBS: Ondansetron", PBS + "ondansetron")],
 [("Gastro", "/gastro/")])

med("iron-tablets", "Iron Tablets: How to Take Them, Alternate-Day Dosing and Side Effects",
 "Iron tablets explained: treating iron deficiency, how much elemental iron, alternate-day dosing for better absorption, vitamin C, tea and calcium, constipation, black stools and when to consider an infusion.",
 "Iron tablets", "Iron tablets",
 [("s", "Iron supplement"), ("l", "Pharmacy and supermarket (unscheduled/S2)")],
 "Cheap and effective for iron deficiency, if you take them in a way your gut can absorb and tolerate.",
 "Iron tablets rebuild iron stores and haemoglobin. Studies show taking a dose once daily or on alternate days absorbs as well or better than several doses a day, with fewer side effects, because a dose raises hepcidin, a hormone that blocks absorption for about a day. Check the elemental iron content: brands vary a lot.",
 [("For", "Iron deficiency with or without anaemia; heavy periods, pregnancy, low intake"),
  ("Works", "Haemoglobin rises about 10 to 20 g/L in 2 to 4 weeks; stores need about 3 months"),
  ("Cost", "About $10 to $20 a month"),
  ("Kicks in", "Feel better in 2 to 4 weeks"),
  ("Dose", "Often 60 to 100 mg elemental iron once daily or on alternate days"),
  ("Watch for", "Constipation, nausea, black stools (harmless)")],
 "Less often can mean more absorbed.",
 ["Ferro-Grad C, Ferro-Tab, FGF, Maltofer, Ferro-Liquid; check the elemental iron per tablet"],
 "Iron is absorbed in the upper small bowel. After a dose, the liver releases hepcidin, which temporarily reduces absorption of the next dose.",
 ["Iron deficiency (low ferritin)", "Iron deficiency anaemia", "Pregnancy (when low or as advised)", "After blood loss"],
 "Gut side effects are the main reason people stop.",
 [g(V, ["Black stools (harmless)"]), g(C, ["Constipation", "Nausea, tummy pain", "Diarrhoea"]), g(R, ["<b>Poisoning in children</b> who swallow adult tablets: emergency"])],
 ["A child swallows iron tablets: call Poisons Information 13 11 26 or 000", "Black, sticky, tarry stools with dizziness (could be bleeding)"],
 ["Take on an empty stomach if you can, or with a little food if it upsets you.", "Vitamin C (or orange juice) can help absorption; avoid tea, coffee, calcium and antacids within 2 hours.", "Alternate-day dosing is a good option if side effects are a problem.", "Recheck ferritin and haemoglobin after about 3 months; ask why you’re low (periods, diet, coeliac disease, bleeding)."],
 [("Is it better to take iron every second day?", "Studies in iron-deficient women found alternate-day dosing absorbed as much or more iron overall, with fewer side effects, than daily doses."),
  ("Why are my stools black on iron?", "Unabsorbed iron turns stools black. It’s harmless. Black, sticky stools with dizziness need urgent review."),
  ("When is an iron infusion better?", "If tablets don’t work, aren’t tolerated, or iron is needed quickly (e.g. late pregnancy, before surgery, or absorption problems).")],
 [("Healthdirect: Iron deficiency", "https://www.healthdirect.gov.au/iron-deficiency")],
 [("Iron", "/iron/"), ("Iron infusion", "/iron-infusion/"), ("Heavy periods", "/heavy-periods/")])

med("vitamin-d-supplements", "Vitamin D Supplements: Doses for Low Vitamin D (Australian Guidance)",
 "Vitamin D supplements explained: deficiency levels in nmol/L, treatment doses (3,000 to 5,000 IU daily for 6 to 12 weeks for moderate deficiency), maintenance, who needs testing and safety.",
 "Vitamin D supplements", "Vitamin D supplements",
 [("s", "Cholecalciferol (vitamin D3)"), ("l", "Pharmacy and supermarket")],
 "Treatment doses for a low level are often higher than people expect: Australian guidance suggests 3,000 to 5,000 IU a day for 6 to 12 weeks for moderate deficiency.",
 "Vitamin D supports bone and muscle health. Australian and New Zealand guidance sets adequacy at 50 nmol/L or more (end of winter). Mild deficiency is 30 to 49 nmol/L, moderate 12.5 to 29, and severe below 12.5. Most people then need about 1,000 to 2,000 IU a day to maintain a normal level.",
 [("For", "Vitamin D deficiency; people at risk (little sun, dark skin, covered clothing, housebound, older age, malabsorption)"),
  ("Works", "Restores levels in 2 to 3 months"),
  ("Cost", "A few dollars a month; 1,000 IU capsules are the common size"),
  ("Kicks in", "Recheck the level after about 3 months"),
  ("Dose", "Moderate to severe deficiency: 3,000 to 5,000 IU daily for at least 6 to 12 weeks; maintenance about 1,000 to 2,000 IU daily (guidelines suggest; check with your doctor)"),
  ("Watch for", "Very high doses long term (toxicity is rare)")],
 "More than you think to fix it, less to keep it.",
 ["Ostelin, Blackmores, Nature’s Way and many others; some combined with calcium"],
 "Vitamin D3 is converted in the liver and kidneys to the active hormone, which increases calcium absorption and supports bone mineralisation and muscle function.",
 ["Treating vitamin D deficiency", "Preventing deficiency in people at risk", "Alongside osteoporosis treatment", "Pregnancy and breastfeeding when levels are low"],
 "At recommended doses it’s very safe.",
 [g(R, ["<b>High calcium</b> from long-term very high doses (nausea, thirst, confusion, kidney stones)"])],
 ["Excessive thirst, confusion or vomiting on high doses", "Low levels that don’t rise with treatment: ask about coeliac disease or other malabsorption"],
 ["Example: a level of 28 nmol/L is moderate deficiency, so guidance suggests 3,000 to 5,000 IU daily for 6 to 12 weeks, then maintenance.", "Take with a meal containing fat.", "Sensible sun exposure helps, balanced against skin cancer risk (Australia has very high UV).", "Routine testing isn’t needed for most healthy people; testing is for those at risk."],
 [("How much vitamin D should I take for a low level?", "Australian and New Zealand guidance suggests 3,000 to 5,000 IU daily for at least 6 to 12 weeks for moderate to severe deficiency (below 30 nmol/L), then around 1,000 to 2,000 IU daily. Check your plan with your doctor."),
  ("What is a normal vitamin D level?", "50 nmol/L or above at the end of winter is considered adequate in Australia."),
  ("Can you take too much vitamin D?", "Yes, but toxicity usually needs very high doses for a long time. Stick to recommended doses.")],
 [("MJA 2012: Vitamin D and health in adults in Australia and New Zealand: a position statement", "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2012/196/11/vitamin-d-and-health-adults-australia-and-new-zealand-position-statement"), ("Australian Prescriber: Vitamin D deficiency in adults", "https://www.nps.org.au/australian-prescriber/articles/vitamin-d-deficiency-in-adults-1")],
 [("Vitamin D", "/vitamin-d/"), ("Osteoporosis", "/osteoporosis/"), ("Bisphosphonates", "/medicines/bisphosphonates/")])
